        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "supporting cmdwin from `<c-r>=` looks feasible, for the \"interactive\" case (insert-mode, cmdline, but _not_ mappings/macros).\n\nsupporting `input()` is in-scope, but hard, because it's \"programmatic\", not \"interactive\". it basically requires the same internal changes as supporting interactive `:!`  https://github.com/neovim/neovim/issues/1496 \n\n### Plan: `<C-F>` cmdwin from `input()` prompt via `:terminal`:\n\n\n1. when `<c-f>` is received during `input()` prompt: wait (spin the event-loop, `LOOP_PROCESS_EVENTS_UNTIL`).\n3. call `run_nvim_in_terminal()`: opens a terminal buffer; spawns child `nvim` in \"cmdwin mode\".\n    - The helper doesn't call eval/reenter the suspended `input()`. The event-loop will still dispatch async callbacks (timers, RPC requests, possibly `TermOpen`). That's not new (timers already fire during a plain `input()`), and it's fine. \n    - To get strict isolation during the modal run, we could gate it with a `textlock`-style flag...\n4. `while (child alive) { loop_poll_events(...); forward input → child pty; }` (render happens automatically via the spin)\n5. on child-exit, reads the result (RPC/temp file), destroys the terminal,\n6. returns the string: the `input()` frame resumes and returns it.\n\nimpl:\n\n- https://github.com/neovim/neovim/pull/40463",
