        {
            "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
            "body": "I tried out 502adbc4116aad879bce80212f7152bd61f5e010 (commit message `WIP: \":read :term\"`). Basic `c-f` functionality is there - thanks! In my very limited testing I haven't faced any problem.\n\nI noticed I can do `CTRL-W n` do open a new window. I suppose that should be disabled\n\n<img width=\"1289\" height=\"1044\" alt=\"Image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/0893a0c9-f19d-4a56-b035-46e81a75e6a5\" />\n\nI like that `:q!` in that cmdwin brings me back to the \"regular rename\" experience, instead of cancelling the whole flow.\n\nI did notice that I don't have all my mappings in that buffer, though. https://github.com/chrisgrieser/nvim-spider (that I use for moving by subwords) or https://github.com/chrisgrieser/nvim-various-textobjs (for `ciS` to rename a subword) are both missing, even though they normally apply to all normal buffers. Both of them are what made it easy for me to rename e.g. `cmdwinWasGreat` to `cmdwinIsGreat` (`\\bciSIs<Esc>` - jump back subword to `Was`, and change `Was` to `Is` - easy with these plugins). I haven't had problems with these mappings in the previous cmdwin implementation",
