        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> My use case is a personal user command `:Compile` that prompts for input. I use `c-f` to edit the command line arguments or to browse/search through my input history via `?`\n\n? \"`c-f` from cmdline\" and \"`c-f` from the `?` search prompt\" already works on master, no changes needed. Unless your `:Compile` command is calling `input()` , which is different.\n\n> I feel an approach like [#40463](https://github.com/neovim/neovim/pull/40463) is a little hacky.\n\nThe old approach was also a hack (globals + special cases + limitations sprinkled throughout the codebase). The new approach is a general solution that opens the door for enabling synchronous behavior for *any* \"async picker\".\n\n> As a compromise, what if we had a `vim.ui.input` implementation that provides something similar to `c-f`?\n\nYour snippet works for code using `vim.ui.input()` (async). But it won't work for `vim.fn.input()` (synchronous), thus it doesn't remove the need for https://github.com/neovim/neovim/pull/40463 .\n\nEnhancing the default behavior of `vim.ui.input()` is something we can explore of course. But it's orthogonal.",
